00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.730
And so just a brief
background on how the body

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.630
elicits an immune response to SARS-CoV-2.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:31.670
So the virus SARS-CoV-2 as seen here,

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.630
it's an RNA virus which
you see in the center

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:37.120
and it's protected by a lipid bilayer.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.070
Within that lipid bilayer,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.290
there are a number of different proteins,

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.470
but there are also these surface proteins,

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.900
in this case, it's spike protein

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.280
that not only elicit our
immune response to the virus,

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.230
but is also necessary for binding

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.020
to the receptor on your cell

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.010
in order for entry and
replication to occur.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:59.050
Now, once the virus binds the
cell and enters and replicate,

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.830
it can cause an immune response.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:04.340
So not only is an immune
response initiated

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.470
towards the surface proteins,

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:09.170
but an immune response can
be made from the proteins

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:11.870
that protect the RNA,
like nuclear proteins

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:15.123
or other proteins within
the matrix of the virus.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.070
Now our immune system is very complex,

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.880
and I'm only gonna talk about
one part of the immune system,

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.570
which is adaptive immunity,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.260
but there's also innate immunity

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.520
which is like the first line
of defense, it's nonspecific,

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.010
it just targets and tries
to eliminate anything

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.920
that's foreign inside of the body.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.770
Unlike, innate immunity,
adaptive immunity,

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.380
recognize specific antigens.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.580
So what occurs, we have a
virus which is a pathogen,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.970
so in this case we have SARS-CoV-2,

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:47.580
SARS-CoV-2 enters our cells, replicates,

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.350
our body recognizes it
as something foreign,

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.460
and so that foreign antigen

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.450
triggers an immune
response within your body.

00:05:56.450 --> 00:06:00.900
And so that immune response
can go on to do several things.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.960
And so the immune response basically

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.900
recognizes this pathogen

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.370
and recognizes certain
parts of the pathogen

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:09.050
which we call antigens.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.030
And so you can either
elicit an immune response

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:14.350
where different cells have
certain receptors on there

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:18.810
to bring about many other immune
cells to combat infection,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.600
or we can make these
and or we can make these

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.240
specialized molecules called antibodies

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.260
that is used to protect us in the future.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.630
Now these antibodies are fairly specific,

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.680
so they recognize specific
parts of the antigen

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.293
where they combine and
eliminate infection.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.520
And so the paper that I'm
gonna talk about today

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.070
focuses on this part
of the immune response

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:42.060
which is a production of antibodies

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.293
that's specific to SARS-CoV-2.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.320
So how does the antibody-antigen work?

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.070
And so within adaptive immunity,

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.060
you have a number of different antibodies

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.870
that are produced by the human body.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.680
There are actually five
different types of antibodies.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.680
We have IgM, IgG, IgD, IgE and IgA.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:04.650
For our purposes today,

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.123
I'm only gonna focus on two antibodies

00:07:06.123 --> 00:07:08.400
that I'm gonna talk
about in the next slide.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.400
But these antibodies recognizes

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:13.840
different parts of the virus.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:17.310
So here's SARS-CoV-2
after it enters the cell.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.380
And so these antibodies that are produced

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.890
can then bind specific parts of the virus,

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.980
and in this case, the spike protein

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.180
and work to either
recruit other immune cells

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.960
to get rid of infection
or neutralize the virus

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.093
so that it doesn't infect your cells.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.860
So what are these two major antibodies

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.530
that seems to be implicated
in SARS-CoV-2 infection?

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:42.650
So we have IgM, so IgM is
one of the first antibodies

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:43.974
to come into play.

00:07:43.974 --> 00:07:48.370
Your body encounters a foreign
antigen or foreign particle,

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:50.860
it recognizes that it
does not belong there.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.850
And so IgM actually
exists off of the cell,

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.330
so if it's not on a cell as a receptor,

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:58.500
it exists alone as a pentamer.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.200
So a pentamer is just
meaning five molecules

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.890
linked together, and once these molecules

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:04.870
enter the bloodstream,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:07.860
it sends messages to the
rest of the immune system

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.720
to produce other antibodies,
and in this case,

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.620
it usually produces, helps
to produce IgG antibodies.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.980
Now IgG antibodies are the
most abundant in antibodies

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:21.350
where you can detect
in humans blood samples

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:22.510
within the serum.

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:27.293
And so IgG exists as a
monomer, meaning one molecule.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.090
All right, so our first poll,

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:37.090
which antibody type is thought
to provoke long term immunity

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:39.341
in humans to COVID-19?

00:08:39.341 --> 00:08:43.223
IgM, IgG, IgD or IgE?

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:46.663
Give you a couple of seconds to answer.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.383
You have five more seconds.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.313
Awesome, so let's see what you thought.

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.160
All right, so 48% thought it was IgG,

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.650
coming into a close second is IgM,

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.950
what about 36% of the vote?

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.100
So the majority actually wins.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:29.370
And so it's actually thought
that IgG is the antibody

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.490
that is thought to provide immunity

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.873
for a next infection to that same virus.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:43.650
All right, so one of those key terms

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:47.150
I'm gonna talk about right
now, which is seroconversion.

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:48.690
What is seroconversion?

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:51.140
So seroconversion is that time point

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:55.250
where you go from being
undetectable for a specific antibody

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:57.590
to being able to detect that antibody

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:00.010
within that patient's blood sample.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:03.180
And so here on this graph,
we have on the y-axis,

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:05.930
we have the time course of infection

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.440
and then on the left we
have the amount present.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:12.150
So usually it takes some
time from being infected

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.710
for our bodies to seroconvert

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.100
to producing a certain
amount of antibodies.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.400
So you can say at this time point,

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.520
this patient seroconverted IgM antibodies

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.990
and at this time point, they
seroconverted IgG antibodies.

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:28.190
And I'll talk a lot more
about seroconversion

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:29.763
in the presentation.

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:34.110
Now another thing that
this study points out

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:36.770
is the different types
of datasets that we have.

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:40.890
And so we can either look at
datasets as aggregate data.

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.600
And so this involves many
different data points,

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.950
it can show trends and comparisons

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:48.690
using statistical analysis,

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.500
but it doesn't look at the individual.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:54.600
And so that's where individual
data comes into play.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.870
And so sometimes when we're
looking at aggregate datasets,

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:59.010
there might be individuals

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.560
that may respond slightly differently.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:06.560
And so a lot of times scientists
zone into the individual

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:09.990
to try to figure out how we can develop

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:13.833
personalized approaches
to that person's response.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:18.610
And so what's the question?

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:21.170
Like what did this paper go in to describe

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:22.480
and try to answer?

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:24.435
And so there's a lot of,

00:11:24.435 --> 00:11:25.750
there's not much information on there

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:30.600
on exactly how our antibodies
respond to COVID-19

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:33.610
or how the immune system
responds to COVID-19.

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.050
And so this is a graph, I've
also used this the last time

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.480
when I was describing the tests

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.430
that we use to develop antibodies.

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:41.340
And so there are still some questions

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.680
that we're trying to understand.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.120
So one of the first goals
that authors look into

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:51.580
is when do we see seroconversion
and how long does it last?

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:55.060
So when can we see or when can we detect

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:57.970
IgM antibodies in a patient's sample?

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:02.350
When can we detect IgG
antibodies in a patient's sample?

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:06.920
How long does the presence of IgM occurs?

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:10.950
And also how long does IgG stay

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:14.390
after someone is infected with COVID-19?

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:17.233
But the authors also wanted
to look at something else.

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:21.570
So we also try to, the
authors also try to decide

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:24.990
if antibody testing can
complement viral testing.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.300
And so I briefly mentioned it,

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.970
but in the beginning you can actually use

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.510
a specific test called RT-PCR.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:34.620
And I want this right,
but now, but if you want,

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:37.560
you can go back to Fran's D4P presentation

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:38.780
where she goes into detail

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:42.000
as to how scientists can
take the genetic information,

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:47.000
change it into a way that we
can detect it in a patient.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:49.400
And so we use RT-PCR to detect

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:51.120
if there are any viral samples

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.310
or viral particles within that patient

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:55.760
who may be currently infected.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:58.960
Another test that we use is
antibody testing such as ELISA,

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:01.030
which I've mentioned it
in the first D4P talks,

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:02.970
so feel free to also check that out.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.500
And where we look at the presence

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.870
or absence of either of these antibodies.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.640
So does someone have IgM antibodies,

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.520
do they have IgG antibodies?

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.100
Have they recovered from infection

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.823
or are they currently
infected with SARS-CoV-2?

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:22.730
So for this study, I'm gonna
describe the patient design

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:24.640
or how they were able to design the study

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:26.920
with the patients that they had.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:30.870
So this is study incorporated 285 patients

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:34.500
from Chongqing, China, and
there were three hospitals

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:37.360
that they were able to get patients from.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:40.300
Out of these 285 patients,

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:44.070
250 of them had some type
of epidemiological history.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.300
So what that means is that these patients

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:48.143
either were residents of Wuhan,

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.470
recently have been to Wuhan

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.410
or have come into close contact
with a resident of Wuhan

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:57.253
or someone who have confirmed COVID-19.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.063
Out of those 285 patients,

00:14:01.063 --> 00:14:04.340
262 of them had clear symptom onset.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:06.200
So what that means is that these patients

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.000
knew exactly when their symptoms began.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.380
So they were hospitalized,

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:12.150
the doctor asks them their history

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:13.910
and they could have told the doctor,

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:16.010
I experienced fatigue or headache

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:17.873
or high fever at a certain day.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:23.380
And out of that 285 patient
dataset, we have 70 of them

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.330
where the scientists were able to gather

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:29.320
multiple serum samples to
look at the seroconversion

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:31.920
that I'm gonna talk about
later in the presentation.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:37.030
So one of the first questions
that the scientists ask are

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:39.730
when are antibodies produced

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:42.740
after someone's infected with SARS-CoV-2?

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.570
And so scientists are
looking at the presence

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:48.140
of these antibodies that
they're able to detect

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:51.000
after these patients
started having symptoms.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:52.690
So what we're looking at

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:57.690
is when patients are
producing IgM antibodies

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:00.550
and when they're producing IgG antibodies,

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:02.760
and how long after their symptoms

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:04.973
are these antibodies being produced?

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:10.870
So to answer this question,
they used 262 patients,

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:14.820
of these patients, they
had 363 serum samples.

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:16.900
And so serum is just part of the blood

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:19.550
that are, we can detect the antibodies.

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:21.500
And so they have 363

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:24.400
because they have multiple
samples from certain patients

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.120
that I've described before.

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:27.500
Now from these samples,

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:32.500
they use a type of ELISA to
detect the presence of the IgG

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:35.150
or IgM antibodies.

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:37.320
And so what did they see?

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.480
So here in this graph I wanna
highlight a couple of things.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.190
So let's zone in on the x-axis.

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:44.120
And so here we see the days

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.560
after the patients
started having symptoms.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.440
So we have dataset from
about two to four days

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:52.780
post having symptoms to about 23 days

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.070
after they started having symptoms.

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:58.900
On the y-axis, what we're
looking at is a positive rate.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:02.500
So how many of these
patients are we able to see

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:06.690
that detect, that we can
detect either IgM, IgG

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.600
or both of these antibodies.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.830
So we're not looking at
the amount of antibodies

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:15.830
but just if they can detect it
or if they cannot detect it.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.230
And so what we're seeing at about 20 days

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:22.500
after patients have symptoms,
you have about 94% of them

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:26.093
where you can detect the IgM antibodies.

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:32.720
And at about 17 days post symptoms onset

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:34.533
a 100% of the patients samples,

00:16:34.533 --> 00:16:37.600
they were able to detect IgG antibodies.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:39.290
But what this chart doesn't show us

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:43.150
is the amount of antibodies
present within these patients

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.123
that have contracted COVID-19.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:48.740
And so next the scientists wanted to see

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:50.980
what the levels were of these antibodies

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:52.383
within these patients.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:58.530
So for this dataset, we
are using 262 patients.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:03.260
And so I'm gonna show you the
first dataset that they show

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:05.280
where on the bottom of the x-axis,

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:06.890
N is the number of patients

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:09.630
that they're assessing
under each time point,

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:13.570
above the N number, you have
the days after symptoms onset.

00:17:13.570 --> 00:17:16.100
So here we have within the first week,

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:19.330
within the second week,
so on and so forth.

00:17:19.330 --> 00:17:22.860
On the y-axis, we're looking
at the levels of antibodies,

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:25.250
and just a reminder
that this is a log scale

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:29.450
so it's exponentially more
as you go up the scale.

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:34.150
And so the authors also
do statistical analysis

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:35.520
to show a number of things.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:37.430
So I also wanna point out
that this is an example

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:38.720
of aggregate data

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:41.060
that includes individual data points.

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:43.510
So all of these dots are
the individual patients,

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:48.690
but we're using statistics to
look at the median or the mean

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:51.370
of all of the data points together

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:54.790
so we can look at the aggregate
data or the data on a whole.

00:17:54.790 --> 00:17:57.300
And so this box indicates the first

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:59.770
and third percentile of patients,

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:02.010
the bar within the middle of the box

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:05.210
look at the average of all
of those patients together,

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:06.630
and then we have the whiskers.

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.700
So anything outside of the whiskers,

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:11.220
we can't say that these
patients were outliers

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:12.513
within that sample set.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:17.050
So this is IgG that we're
looking at, and what you can see

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:20.060
if we're looking at the
averages of all of the data

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:23.130
is that you see a continuous
increase in the levels

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:27.600
of IgG antibodies up to
about past three weeks

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:29.730
post symptoms onset.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:33.020
So the authors also looked at the amount

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:36.890
of IgM antibodies levels in the patients.

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:39.370
And so what you see here, is on the bottom

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.460
we have the number of patients
that they're looking at

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:45.880
and the days after they
started having symptoms.

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:48.670
And so here you can see as well

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:50.490
when we're looking at IgG,

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.530
the averages of this aggregate data

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:56.550
you see a trend of an
increase of IgM antibodies

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.800
in terms of the levels that we're seeing,

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:01.790
and after three weeks you
start to see a slight decline

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:02.840
of that levels.

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.410
Once again, there are some
individual data points

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:06.760
that are outliers,

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:10.140
but in order to get a picture
of the population on a whole,

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:12.410
we have to use this aggregate dataset

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:15.650
to look at the averages so we can infer

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:19.313
how the population reacts
to a certain stimulus.

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:22.340
So overall this slide is showing

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.590
that we're seeing a slight decrease in IgM

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:26.780
after three weeks.

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:28.840
So what does this change what we know?

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:31.290
So in the first graph
that I've been presenting,

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:32.950
I'm showing that initially

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:36.100
you're seeing IgM
antibodies being detected,

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:38.290
and then you're seeing
IgG antibodies detected

00:19:38.290 --> 00:19:41.980
with IgM decreasing as IgG increases.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:44.170
Now this changes the graph a little.

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:45.820
So what we're starting to see is that

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:49.890
it seems that antibodies are
produced around the same time

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:51.450
and after about three weeks or so

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:56.197
you're seeing a slight decrease
in antibody levels for IgM,

00:19:56.197 --> 00:19:59.970
but you're seeing a
continual increase in IgG,

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:01.810
and the authors also point out that

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:06.080
some of the patient's samples
started to remain steady

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.543
or plateau throughout the time point.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:14.000
Now there's a lot of
talk about whether or not

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:17.180
a patient has a more
severe case of COVID-19

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:19.240
if they produce more antibodies

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:22.290
than someone who has a less
severe case of CIVID-19

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:24.950
or are asymptomatic to infection.

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:28.280
And so what the authors
do to combat this question

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:31.340
is that they looked at
a subset of patients

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:33.150
who they classified as severe.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:37.010
So severe would be someone who's
been admitted into the ICU.

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:41.080
And so out of the patient
cohort, they had about 20

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:44.470
that they classified as having severe.

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:46.950
So the orange dots are severe cases,

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:49.630
the green are non-severe cases.

00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:51.430
And you're also looking at the time

00:20:51.430 --> 00:20:53.770
after the patient started having symptoms.

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.650
So here in this portion of the graph,

00:20:56.650 --> 00:20:58.200
we're looking at the first week

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:00.350
after patients started to have symptoms,

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:02.120
and here we're looking at the second week

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.180
after they started to have symptoms.

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:06.010
Now this dataset shows

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:08.470
we're gonna zone in on the median

00:21:08.470 --> 00:21:10.840
of this entire patient dataset,

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:15.380
and we're seeing that there
seems to be a trend of people

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.800
who have severe cases of COVID-19

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:22.620
have higher levels of IgG
antibodies being produced,

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:25.960
unlike those who have non-severe cases.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:26.870
But also I wanna point out

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:29.150
if we were to zone in
on the individual data

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:31.410
that we do have some patients

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:34.520
who are also producing
large amounts of antibodies

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:38.163
and even though that they
don't have severe symptoms.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:41.120
They looked at the same thing

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:43.910
post two weeks of symptoms onset

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:48.200
and you do see a slight
increase of antibodies produced

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:50.760
in comparison to those
who have non-severe.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:52.910
The only significant
difference that they show

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:55.350
is within that first week with IgG.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.410
And so what the P values indicate

00:21:57.410 --> 00:22:02.150
is how much that this phenomenon
is occurring by chance,

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:07.150
and scientists pick a number
which is P equals 0.05,

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:08.970
and if that number is less than that,

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:11.410
we can say that the
chances of that happening

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.560
by random chance is very low,

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:15.050
and it actually does have something

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:18.120
to do with the phenomenon
that they're looking at.

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:20.800
The authors also looked at IgM.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:23.480
And so here again, if
we're looking at the median

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:26.620
for each of the datasets, we
see within the first week,

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:28.610
you do see a slight increase

00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:32.410
of the amount of antibodies
produced for severe patients,

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:34.920
and the same is true for those

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:37.430
post two weeks of symptoms onset.

00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:39.340
Once again, this is a small dataset,

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:43.010
so we can only come up with conclusions

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:45.433
based on the sample size that we have.

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:49.560
So what did authors show so far?

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:52.040
So all of the patient's
samples that they've tested.

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:53.560
So looking at their serums

00:22:53.560 --> 00:22:55.883
and looking for detectable antibodies,

00:22:55.883 --> 00:22:59.030
100% of them produce IgG antibodies

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:00.370
about two to three weeks

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:03.090
after they started to have symptoms.

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:06.490
IgM antibodies, the
amount seems to decline

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:09.160
after about three weeks
of having symptoms,

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:10.650
and there seems to be a trend

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.520
where patients with severe symptoms

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:16.310
are producing slightly
higher levels of IgM and IgG

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:17.840
with significance

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:21.700
only seen in the first week post infection

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.133
with IgG antibody levels.

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:28.880
All right, so I'm gonna
dive in a little bit deeper

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:31.370
on the seroconversion of antibodies.

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:34.990
So I mentioned seroconversion
just means being negative

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:36.740
and being able to detect

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:41.300
either the IgM or IgG
in this case antibodies

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:42.693
in a particular patient.

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:46.120
So in order to combat these questions,

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:48.940
the scientists came up with
three different scenarios.

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:52.310
So the first is what we call
synchronous seroconversion.

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:55.200
And so what that means is
that you're able to detect

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:59.460
both IgG and IgM
antibodies at the same time

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:01.143
post the symptoms onset.

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:06.290
The next scenario is if
a patient produces IgM

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:07.630
earlier than IgG,

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:09.950
and this is kind of the
model that scientists use

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:11.710
for other viral infections

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:14.930
where IgM is being detected first,

00:24:14.930 --> 00:24:16.770
followed by IgG antibodies

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:19.903
a couple of days after
they're able to detect IgM.

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:23.440
And the third seroconversion method is

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:27.070
whether or not you can
detect IgG antibodies

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:29.410
earlier than you can detect IgM.

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:33.000
So here you can detect
IgG, a few days later,

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:35.063
you can then detect IgM.

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:41.120
So another poll, which
version of seroconversion

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:44.010
do you think is occurring with patients

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:47.090
who've been infected with COVID-19?

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:49.340
Do you think it's
synchronous seroconversion?

00:24:50.691 --> 00:24:54.200
Do you think it's IgM first and then IgG

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:57.293
or do you think it's IgG
first followed by IgM?

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:07.040
Give you a couple of seconds to answer.

00:25:19.730 --> 00:25:22.463
Awesome, so we'll end the poll.

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:30.180
And so about 75% of the
audience thinks that IgM

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:33.180
are produced first and then IgG,

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:38.180
and then about 20% thinks it's
IgM and IgG being produced

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:41.150
or detected at the same time.

00:25:41.150 --> 00:25:44.190
Now I'm not gonna tell you to answer,

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:45.727
I'm just gonna show you the data.

00:25:45.727 --> 00:25:47.810
(laughs)

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:50.080
All right, so in order for the authors

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.763
to look at this seroconversion
that's happening,

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:55.370
it's a couple of things that they look at.

00:25:55.370 --> 00:25:57.100
So they have 63 patients

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.130
and they're able to have serum samples

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:00.760
in three-day intervals.

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:02.650
Now out of those 63 patients,

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:06.640
96% of them were able to
seroconvert antibodies.

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:10.260
So they were able to
detect either IgG, IgM

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:12.263
or both of these antibodies.

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:15.930
But within that 63 patients,

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:18.270
they were able to gather 27 patients,

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:19.410
when they were hospitalized,

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:21.703
they were initially seronegative.

00:26:21.703 --> 00:26:25.180
So what seronegative means
is that they are unable

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:29.630
to detect the presence
of IgG or IgM antibodies.

00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:34.500
And so they took the sample
and these samples of patients

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:37.610
and they looked at when these patients

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:40.860
are producing either IgM or IgG.

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:43.800
All right, so let's see what
the scientists find out.

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:46.200
So now we're looking
at individual datasets.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:50.230
So each dot again is looking
at each individual person

00:26:50.230 --> 00:26:52.960
producing a certain type of antibodies.

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:54.690
So let's zone in on the x-axis.

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:58.160
So here we're looking at the
days after symptoms onset,

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:01.940
and on the y-axis, we're
just looking at the amount of

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:04.570
either the IgG or the IgM.

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:09.370
The red circles are IgG and
the blue triangles are IgM.

00:27:09.370 --> 00:27:12.020
And so for 10 out of those 27 patients,

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:15.300
they're actually seen that
they're able to detect IgG

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:17.447
before they're able to detect IgM.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:20.440
And the median days of seroconversion

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:24.690
where they can detect these
antibodies is about two weeks

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:27.490
or 13 days post symptoms onset.

00:27:27.490 --> 00:27:29.780
And so what this will look
like is something like this

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:30.830
where you're seeing

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:34.110
or you're being able to
detect IgG antibodies

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:36.570
in comparison to IgM antibodies,

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:38.453
what happens a short time after.

00:27:40.640 --> 00:27:42.060
Next, they were able to show

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:45.060
that nine out of those 27 patients

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:50.060
actually produced IgM and IgG
antibodies at the same time.

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.300
And so on the bottom of
the x-axis is the same.

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:54.920
So you're seeing about six
days after symptoms onset,

00:27:54.920 --> 00:27:57.320
you have patients where you can detect

00:27:57.320 --> 00:27:59.350
both of these antibodies.

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:02.370
Once again, seroconversion
of both of these antibodies

00:28:02.370 --> 00:28:04.960
occur on average about 13 days,

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:06.810
and here's what that graph would look like

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:08.850
where you have the detectable levels

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:12.683
of IgM and IgG antibodies
occurring at the same time.

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:18.910
And then the patients,

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:22.700
the scientists had seven
of those 27 patients

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:26.170
actually produced or
seroconverted IgM antibodies

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:29.420
before they produced IgG antibodies.

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:31.300
Once again, the x-axis is the days

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:34.060
after the patients
started to have symptoms,

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:36.570
where you're able to
detect IgM in antibodies

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:40.340
and later you can detect the
IgG antibodies with 13 days

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:42.940
being that median date of seroconversion.

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:45.980
So this graph would
look something like this

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:48.390
where you're producing IgM antibodies

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:50.280
prior to IgG antibodies.

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:51.670
So the answer to the question

00:28:51.670 --> 00:28:55.140
is that it happens differently
in different patients.

00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:58.360
I think this is another
reason to point out

00:28:58.360 --> 00:29:01.270
sometimes how individual datasets

00:29:01.270 --> 00:29:02.550
actually a super important one

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:05.410
when we're looking at
approaches for medicines

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:13.290
I'm gonna move on to the second goal.

00:29:13.290 --> 00:29:14.630
And so I've shown you

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:18.620
within the sample set
that the scientists have

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:21.280
when they're producing antibodies

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:23.350
and how these antibodies are seroconverted

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:25.070
within these patients.

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:26.720
But now that authors wanna ask,

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:31.040
how can serology testing
help identify COVID-19?

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.620
Now serology testing is
just a fancy way to say

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:36.610
that we're testing for the
presence of antibodies.

00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:38.880
And so I just fine-tuned the graph

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:41.310
based on the data that I gave you.

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:43.100
Remember this is not really a one for all,

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:47.010
but this is just an depiction
of like the aggregate dataset.

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:51.140
And so on the bottom we have
the days post symptoms onset.

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:52.760
And so what you'll see in the beginning

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:55.220
once you have this viral infection,

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:57.563
you can test with RT-PCR,

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:02.780
and you can see if you
have levels of the virus

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:03.740
within that patient

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:06.600
to show that they're currently
infected with COVID-19.

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:09.210
Now a lot of times
patients only get tested

00:30:09.210 --> 00:30:10.670
if they're having symptoms.

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:13.860
So you're feeling crummy,
one day the symptoms occur,

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.440
you're like, oh crap,
this might be COVID-19,

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:19.590
you get the viral tests, they
tell you that you're positive.

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:21.740
But I just wanna remind
you that there are patients

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:24.080
who actually never have symptoms, right?

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:27.210
The only times until recently

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:29.270
where patients would get tested

00:30:29.270 --> 00:30:31.330
is if they've been into
close contact with patients

00:30:31.330 --> 00:30:33.340
who had confirmed COVID-19.

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:35.070
So you have the asymptomatic patients

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:37.460
that may or may not get tested.

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:40.810
Now, if the test is super accurate,

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:43.630
you'll see that they tested positive

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:46.060
for the presence of
the virus in their body

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:48.860
by using the RT-PCR test.

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:53.130
But there are cases where you
can get false negative results

00:30:53.130 --> 00:30:54.450
where the test can tell you

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:56.320
that you're negative for the virus,

00:30:56.320 --> 00:31:00.650
but maybe in actuality you
actually contracted COVID-19

00:31:00.650 --> 00:31:03.880
and you were symptomatic and
you did not know that you had

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:07.240
the presence of that virus in your body.

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:08.950
Now the serological test

00:31:08.950 --> 00:31:11.510
is where we can look for IgM and IgG,

00:31:11.510 --> 00:31:13.870
and looking at the presence
of these antibodies

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:16.820
can allow scientists to figure out

00:31:16.820 --> 00:31:20.200
if a patient have the presence of IgM,

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:22.920
meaning that they have
been infected with COVID-19

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:24.750
or the presence of IgG,

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:26.530
meaning that there are currently infected

00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:30.170
or they've recovered
from COVID-19 infection.

00:31:30.170 --> 00:31:32.210
And so what these authors did

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:34.450
is look at a number of patients

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:36.640
who have come into close contact

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:39.223
with those who have confirmed COVID-19.

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:43.950
And so they have 164 of those patients,

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:48.740
and they took those patients
and they use the RT-PCR test

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:51.937
to look for the virus,
and out of those 164,

00:31:52.787 --> 00:31:57.700
16 of them tested positive
using the RT-PCR test.

00:31:57.700 --> 00:32:01.520
Out of those 16, 13 of
them were symptomatic,

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:05.440
so they had symptoms and three
of them were asymptomatic,

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:10.440
and all of them are tested
positive for IgG or IgM or both.

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:16.340
Now the scientist also did RT-PCR tests

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:19.210
on the rest of the patients,

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:21.380
and these patients had no symptoms,

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:25.470
and they also tested
negative by the RT-PCR test.

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:27.820
Now 30 days after they tested negative,

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.570
they gave all the patients
an antibody tests,

00:32:30.570 --> 00:32:31.880
and to their surprise,

00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:34.980
they were able to see that seven of them,

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:39.980
they were able to detect
IgG and or IgM antibodies

00:32:40.070 --> 00:32:42.170
to SARS-CoV-2.

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:43.760
And so what this is saying is that

00:32:43.760 --> 00:32:46.120
not only did we have 10 patients

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:51.120
out of the 164 close contacts
that were asymptomatic,

00:32:52.300 --> 00:32:56.330
but the RT-PCR missed
about 4% of the patients

00:32:56.330 --> 00:33:00.120
who actually did contract COVID-19.

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:03.020
So what lessons can we get from this

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.713
and what story does this tell?

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:09.920
And so it appears that scientists

00:33:09.920 --> 00:33:14.190
are looking to initiate a way

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:18.680
that we can combine antibody
testing with RT-PCR tests

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:21.430
because it may be critical
to identify patients

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:23.390
who currently have COVID-19

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:25.540
and those who've recovered from infection.

00:33:25.540 --> 00:33:27.960
And so if we're able to
complement the two testings,

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:31.543
this would allow us to increase
the accuracy for infection.

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:36.120
And so they also showed that

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:38.280
all the patients that they looked at

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:40.340
that were infected with COVID-19,

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:44.857
produced or seroconverted
detectable levels of IgG.

00:33:44.857 --> 00:33:47.750
Now this IgG antibody
like I mentioned before,

00:33:47.750 --> 00:33:51.820
is the antibody that scientists
think may infer immunity,

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:55.710
but we're still unsure as to
how long this immunity lasts.

00:33:55.710 --> 00:33:58.530
And if you wanted to learn more about

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:00.150
if you can get reinfected,

00:34:00.150 --> 00:34:04.310
I would like if you can
check out Joe's talk,

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:07.270
who talked about a study that
scientists did in macaques

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:10.730
where they were unable
to reinfect these monkeys

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:14.280
with SARS-CoV-2, but also
we wanna bear in mind

00:34:14.280 --> 00:34:17.573
that this may not necessarily
translate to humans.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:21.730
And they also showed that patients

00:34:21.730 --> 00:34:24.320
produce IgM and IgG antibodies

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:26.740
that can be detected at
different time points

00:34:26.740 --> 00:34:28.610
after they started having symptoms,

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:30.960
but on average, if we're
looking at the aggregate data,

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:32.910
it appears to be about two weeks

00:34:32.910 --> 00:34:35.210
after you started having
symptoms to COVID-19.

00:34:36.730 --> 00:34:38.910
And so I think this is great

00:34:38.910 --> 00:34:40.540
that the study is telling us all of this,

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:43.050
but once again, with
every scientific article

00:34:43.050 --> 00:34:46.240
and every experiment,
there are some limitations.

00:34:46.240 --> 00:34:48.310
And so one, they did not test

00:34:48.310 --> 00:34:50.840
for the presence of
neutralizing antibodies.

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:53.020
What are neutralizing
antibodies you ask?

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:54.360
And so these antibodies

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:58.210
basically have the ability
to bind that specific virus,

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:00.860
coat it so that it's
unable to infect the cells.

00:35:00.860 --> 00:35:02.130
So it gets rid of the virus

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:05.080
even before it comes into
contact with your cells.

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:06.780
Even though we're producing antibodies,

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:08.730
the antibodies may also be necessary

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:11.160
for recruiting other cells

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:13.590
or other molecules to
get rid of infection,

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:15.693
but infection may already be taking place.

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:19.600
A new paper just came out in BioRxiv,

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:21.057
so this is a preprint.

00:35:21.057 --> 00:35:24.500
It came out of Rockefeller
University, my employer.

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:26.260
And so what they're showing

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:29.440
is that the patients that they looked at,

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:31.370
so these are all patients
in New York City,

00:35:31.370 --> 00:35:32.980
this dataset that I'm showing you today

00:35:32.980 --> 00:35:35.637
are all patients from China,

00:35:35.637 --> 00:35:38.100
and from the dataset
that they're looking at,

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:41.700
it appears that only a few people

00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:43.430
who've been affected with SARS-CoV-2

00:35:43.430 --> 00:35:46.270
are producing high amounts
of neutralizing antibodies,

00:35:46.270 --> 00:35:47.900
but it doesn't mean that we're not,

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:49.760
the others are not producing any at all,

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:52.670
it just might be a minute
amount of antibodies

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:54.950
that they're unable to
detect in the laboratory.

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:56.803
So feel free to check out that paper.

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:00.710
Also, I just wanna pinpoint

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:03.520
that the sample size of
this study is fairly small,

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:06.760
so we only look at about 280 patients

00:36:06.760 --> 00:36:10.100
for a lot of the data that I showed you,

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:11.210
but even when we're looking at

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:12.433
severe versus critical patients,

00:36:12.433 --> 00:36:14.150
they were only about 20

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:16.870
that we classified as severe and critical.

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:18.850
And so even though it would show the trend

00:36:18.850 --> 00:36:19.870
with the aggregate data,

00:36:19.870 --> 00:36:22.450
we're still unsure as to how the antibody

00:36:22.450 --> 00:36:25.033
correlates with the clinical
course of infection.

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:28.190
And also this does not look at

00:36:28.190 --> 00:36:30.080
the changes in antibody responses

00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:32.170
for a prolonged period of time.

00:36:32.170 --> 00:36:34.260
Remember the scientists
only had the ability

00:36:34.260 --> 00:36:36.520
to test patients who were hospitalized,

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:41.520
and so once they were able
to leave the hospital,

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:43.090
they're unable to test them,

00:36:43.090 --> 00:36:46.100
so even though we are
producing IgG antibodies,

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:47.690
and they also showed other data

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:49.460
where they seemed to be

00:36:49.460 --> 00:36:51.720
like the IgG antibodies are plateauing,

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:55.190
we can't really say anything
for past about 25 days

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:56.400
post symptoms onset

00:36:56.400 --> 00:36:58.623
because the authors
did not look into that.

